WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:07.830
Now on to AI Perception. Whilst we can leverage the power of the environment query system to gather data about the environment,

2
00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:13.050
the AI Perception provides framework for the AI to receive sensory data.

3
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:19.079
The AI Perception framework gives us developers a tool to gather sensory data for each AI.

4
00:00:19.079 --> 00:00:27.339
As we can see from the slide, Unreal gives us access to certain sensory stimuli such as sight, hearing, touch, damage and even team.

5
00:00:27.339 --> 00:00:31.339
However, this is another system that does not come as default when creating our AI.

6
00:00:31.339 --> 00:00:37.840
In order to leverage this, we need to do some extra steps, the first of which is to add the AI Perception component to our AI Controller.

7
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:44.840
And once again this can be done via Blueprint, as the AI Perception component is Blueprint spawnable, or we could add it directly through the native C++.

8
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.979
As the name suggests, it's the job of the AI Perception component to receive the sensory data,

9
00:00:50.979 --> 00:00:57.329
and it uses that information to inform us as to what has been perceived and by what sense it has been perceived by.

10
00:00:57.329 --> 00:01:01.329
Of course what we do with this functionality beyond this point is completely up to us as engineers.

11
00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:05.329
What we do with this data will depend on the tech spec and design in mind.

12
00:01:05.329 --> 00:01:13.769
A really handy aspect of the AI Perception framework is that it's totally customizable per AI, and it can be altered at runtime.

13
00:01:13.769 --> 00:01:20.269
As we mentioned, the AI Perception component can be added to the AI Controller either through Blueprint or through native C++.

14
00:01:20.269 --> 00:01:26.400
It allows us to define what senses we want our AI to listen out for, as well as configure the parameters of those senses.

15
00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:32.040
We can also define a dominant sense that will take precedence over all of the other ones.

16
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:40.049
It's important to note that this sense must be one of the senses configured in the list of senses config file, otherwise it will be left as None.

17
00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:45.620
The Perception component also exposes out events to Blueprint that we can utilize to drive functionality.

18
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.120
The list of events can be seen on the image to the right, so as an example we have the OnPerceptionUpdated function,

19
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.620
which will be fired when the Perception component processes all of its stimuli, and it will return a list of the recently perceived actors.

20
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:04.939
The AISite stimulus does exactly as you'd expect. It allows the AI to see things in the environment.

21
00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:11.129
The AISiteConfig exposes key variables to developers that allow us to control what an AI agent sees.

22
00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:18.129
When an actor enters a site radius of an AI agent, the Perception system signals an update and passes through the actor that it has seen.

23
00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:22.580
So let's go over some of the properties that we can see in the above image.

24
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:28.580
Firstly we've got the implementation, and this is the AISense class to use, and it will default to AISenseSite.

25
00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.580
You can overwrite this with your own implementation as you see fit.

26
00:02:31.580 --> 00:02:36.900
Next we've got the SiteRadius, and this is the max distance over which this sense can start perceiving.

27
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:45.379
Next we've got the LoseSiteRadius, and this is the maximum distance over which a seen target is no longer perceived by this sense.

28
00:02:45.379 --> 00:02:52.569
Next we've got the PeripheralVisionHalfAngleDegrees, and this is how far to either side the AI can see in degrees.

29
00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:57.759
This is the angle measured in relation to the current forward vector of the AI agent.

30
00:02:57.759 --> 00:03:03.759
Next we've got DetectionByAffiliation, and this determines if enemies, neutrals or friendlies can trigger this sense,

31
00:03:03.759 --> 00:03:08.759
and it's worth noting that this property can only be defined and configured in the native C++.

32
00:03:08.759 --> 00:03:13.500
Next we've got the AutoSuccessRangeFromLastSeenLocation.

33
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:19.719
When put to a value greater than zero, the AI will always be able to see the target that has already been seen,

34
00:03:19.719 --> 00:03:22.719
as long as they are within the specified range.

35
00:03:22.719 --> 00:03:29.039
And lastly we've got the MaxAge, and this will determine the duration in which the stimuli generated by this sense becomes forgotten,

36
00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:32.039
and if left at zero, it will never be forgotten.

37
00:03:32.039 --> 00:03:35.900
Next we have Hearing.

38
00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.900
The AI Hearing sense can be used to detect sounds generated by a report noise event.

39
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.319
An example of this can be, say, the footfalls of the player or a gun being fired.

40
00:03:46.319 --> 00:03:50.319
Again, let's take a look at some of the values that we have available to us in this sense.

41
00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:57.319
Firstly we've got the implementation, and much like the sight implementation, this is simply where we set the class of the sense we want to use.

42
00:03:57.319 --> 00:04:04.860
Next we've got the HearingRange, and this simply limits how far a reported noise event has to be for it to be perceived by this sense.

43
00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:11.659
If the event is fired further away than this value allows, it will not be reported by the perception system.

44
00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:19.269
Next we have the LOS HearingRange, and this is used to display a different radius in the debugger for the HearingRange property.

45
00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:24.269
Following on from that we have Detection by Affiliation, and this is the same as the AI's sight sense.

46
00:04:24.269 --> 00:04:31.269
It will determine if enemies, neutrals or friendlies can trigger this sense, and again, this can only be configured in the native C++.

47
00:04:31.269 --> 00:04:35.660
Finally we also mirror the MaxAge parameter from the sight too.

48
00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:44.230
This determines how long actors detected by this stimuli will be forgotten, and again, if this is set to zero, it will never be forgotten.

49
00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.870
Next we've got the Damage sense.

50
00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:53.870
If we want our AI agents to react to damage being taken, whether that be point or radial, this is the sense that we'll want to use.

51
00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.220
Let's take a look at some of the properties within this sense.

52
00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.769
Firstly we have implementation, and this controls how damage events are handled.

53
00:05:01.769 --> 00:05:08.769
However if you are making your own damage system, then it is highly recommended that you simply make your own implementation for triggering this sense.

54
00:05:08.769 --> 00:05:14.920
Lastly we have MaxAge, and this mirrors every other instance of MaxAge we've seen thus far.

55
00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:19.920
This essentially determines how long an actor detected by this stimuli will remain in memory.

56
00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.920
If this is set to zero, they will never be forgotten.

57
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:29.660
You'll also notice that we don't mirror the Detection by Affiliation properties that we have with sight and with hearing.

58
00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:38.459
With a damage input, we should endeavor to make sure we don't damage the actor in the first place if they are an ally, neutral or enemy.

59
00:05:38.459 --> 00:05:44.459
We should have some kind of implementation before we even get to this point to prevent the damage being from administered in the first place.

60
00:05:44.459 --> 00:05:48.769
Following on from damage, we have the TouchSense.

61
00:05:48.769 --> 00:05:54.769
This sense gives us the ability to detect when an ally bumps into something else, or when something bumps into it.

62
00:05:54.769 --> 00:06:00.769
A great example of this sense would be to allow an ally agent to have knowledge of a player if they bump into them while sneaking around.

63
00:06:00.769 --> 00:06:09.500
We don't have a great deal of parameters that we can adjust for this particular sense, however we can still adjust the MaxAge which we've covered in the last few slides.

64
00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:13.139
Lastly we have the TeamSense.

65
00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:19.139
This sense will notify the perception system when someone on the same team as our AI agent is nearby.

66
00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:26.240
This can be useful in situations in which we want our AI agent to react to its allies gathering around them, maybe to perform some kind of group attack.

67
00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:33.240
Once again we can see we've got much of the same set of properties that we've already discussed in the previous slides, most notably again the MaxAge.

68
00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:41.430
So at this point we can configure our AI agent to have a perception component and even give it the senses it will use to gather information about the world.

69
00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:47.430
But there's still the last crucial part of the system that we need to talk about, and that is the AI Stimulus Source component.

70
00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:54.069
This component gives the owning actor a way to automatically register itself as a stimulus source.

71
00:06:54.069 --> 00:06:58.649
This component can be added in both Blueprint and in C++.

72
00:06:58.649 --> 00:07:09.649
With this component we can dictate what senses we register ourselves to, or by simply entering each sense into the Register as Source for Senses array, which is exposed out to the Editor Details panel.
